<p>A fire changes everything in minutes. One moment it is a normal day at home or at work, and the next you are dealing with smoke-filled rooms, ruined belongings, structural questions, and a stack of insurance paperwork. During that stretch, the <strong>fire insurance adjuster</strong> becomes one of the most important people in your recovery, and it matters a great deal which side that adjuster is on.</p>

<p>This guide explains the different adjusters involved in a fire claim, why fire losses are more complex than they look, and what homeowners and business owners in Corpus Christi and the Coastal Bend should do in the first days and weeks.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Fire-Insurance-Adjuster-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster in a white hard hat taking notes inside the charred framing of a burned house" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Structural members that look sound may still be weakened by heat. A careful inspection checks framing, not just finishes.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What is a fire insurance adjuster?</h2>

<p>A fire insurance adjuster evaluates fire-related losses and helps determine what is owed under an insurance policy. The key detail many property owners miss is that adjusters do not all work for the same side.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of adjuster</th><th>Represents</th><th>Main role</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Staff (company) adjuster</td><td>The insurance company</td><td>Evaluates the claim under company guidelines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Independent adjuster</td><td>The insurance company that hired them</td><td>Handles claims on the insurer’s behalf</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public adjuster</td><td>The policyholder</td><td>Documents, values, and presents the claim for the owner</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Fire claims involve many judgment calls. How far did smoke travel? Can these materials be cleaned, or must they be replaced? Is the framing still sound? What will the rebuild really cost here? Reasonable professionals can reach different answers, which is why it helps to know whose interests each person represents. Our article on <a href="/questions-about-public-adjusters/">common questions about public adjusters</a> covers this in more depth.</p>

<h2>Why fire claims are more complex than most property claims</h2>

<p>Visible burn damage is often only part of the loss. Smoke and soot move through wall cavities, attics, and ductwork. Heat can weaken wiring, windows, and framing that look intact. Water from firefighting soaks drywall, insulation, and flooring, and in Corpus Christi’s humidity, wet materials can grow mold quickly if drying is delayed. A small kitchen fire can affect the whole house. A minor electrical fire in a business can contaminate an entire HVAC system.</p>

<p>A single fire claim may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Structural damage to framing, roof, and slab-on-grade foundations</li>
<li>Smoke and soot contamination throughout the building</li>
<li>Water damage from firefighting</li>
<li>Electrical and HVAC damage</li>
<li>Personal property (contents) losses</li>
<li>Loss of use or additional living expenses</li>
<li>Business income loss for commercial properties</li>
</ul>

<p>Each category needs its own documentation. Leaving one out can leave money on the table. Our <a href="/services/smoke-damage-claims/">smoke damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> pages go deeper on those parts of a fire loss.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Fire-Insurance-Adjuster-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Collage of fire claim steps: boarding up windows, inspecting charred rooms, photographing damage, preparing estimates, and reviewing the policy" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A fire claim moves from emergency board-up to inspection, documentation, policy review, and negotiation.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What happens after a fire insurance claim is filed</h2>

<h3>Step 1: Safety and emergency mitigation</h3>
<p>Do not enter the building until the fire department says it is safe. Then secure the property: board up openings, tarp the roof, and stop further water or weather damage. Most policies require reasonable steps to protect the property. Keep every receipt and photograph the damage before and after mitigation.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Report the claim and request the policy</h3>
<p>Report promptly and ask for a complete copy of your policy, including endorsements. Under Texas Insurance Code §542.055, an insurer generally must acknowledge the claim, start its investigation, and request what it needs within 15 days of notice.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Inspection</h3>
<p>The insurer’s adjuster will inspect the structure, contents, and related damage. Be there, walk every room, and point out smoke odor and staining in areas the fire never reached.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Documentation</h3>
<p>Photos and video of every room, ceiling, and closet; contractor and remediation estimates; engineering reports if structure is in question; receipts; and a detailed contents inventory. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can keep this organized.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Decision, payment, and negotiation</h3>
<p>The insurer must generally accept or reject the claim within 15 business days after receiving all requested items, or 30 days if it has reason to believe the loss resulted from arson (§542.056). Once it accepts, payment is generally due within 5 business days (§542.057). After a declared weather catastrophe these deadlines can be extended 15 days, but that rarely applies to a single-building fire.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Texas prompt-payment step</th><th>Deadline (private insurers)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge claim, begin investigation, request items</td><td>15 days after notice (§542.055)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject</td><td>15 business days after receiving all requested items; 30 days if arson is suspected (§542.056)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension if insurer needs more time</td><td>Must give reasons, then decide within 45 days (§542.056)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay after acceptance</td><td>5 business days (§542.057)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>An arson investigation on your own loss can feel personal. It is a routine step in many fire claims. Cooperate, answer questions accurately, and if you are asked to give an examination under oath or a recorded statement and you have concerns, talk to a Texas attorney.</p>

