<h2>How fire damage is evaluated</h2>

<p><strong>Structure.</strong> Heat can compromise roof framing, floor systems, load-bearing walls, and windows before anything visibly fails. When in doubt, an engineer’s report helps settle what must be replaced.</p>

<p><strong>Smoke and soot.</strong> Smoke settles in insulation, drywall, cabinets, furniture, electronics, and ductwork. Cleaning that does not remove odor and residue is not a complete repair, and the estimate should reflect the cleaning or replacement actually needed.</p>

<p><strong>Water.</strong> Firefighting water affects flooring, ceilings, insulation, and electrical systems. Prompt drying limits mold, and the drying work itself belongs in the claim.</p>

<p><strong>Contents.</strong> Most people underestimate their belongings until they start listing them. Go room by room, including closets, garages, and storage. Record description, quantity, age, and approximate replacement cost. Under many replacement cost policies, the insurer pays actual cash value first and the rest after items are replaced, within a deadline in the policy.</p>

<h2>Building a contents inventory that holds up</h2>

<p>The contents inventory is often the most time-consuming part of a fire claim, and one of the most important. A few habits make it much easier for the insurer to review and much harder to dispute:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Work room by room.</strong> Start in one corner and move clockwise. Include closets, cabinets, drawers, the garage, the attic, and outdoor storage.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph before anything is moved.</strong> Wide shots of each room, then close-ups of brand labels and model numbers where you can read them.</li>
<li><strong>Record the details.</strong> For each item: description, brand and model, quantity, approximate age, where it was purchased if you know, and current replacement cost.</li>
<li><strong>Use what you already have.</strong> Old photos, online order histories, bank and credit card statements, warranty cards, and manuals all help establish what you owned.</li>
<li><strong>Separate clean, salvage, and discard.</strong> Smoke-damaged items may be cleanable. Note which items were cleaned, which were replaced, and why.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it in a spreadsheet.</strong> A sortable list is easier to update, total, and send than handwritten pages.</li>
</ol>

<p>Be accurate. Overstating an item’s value or age can damage the credibility of the whole claim, while understating it leaves money unclaimed. If the list is large, it is reasonable to submit it in sections as you finish each room.</p>

<h2>Rebuilding after a fire in Corpus Christi</h2>

<p>The City of Corpus Christi adopted the 2021 International Building, Residential, and Existing Building Codes, with local amendments, effective August 1, 2023. Repairs need permits through Development Services, and code upgrades that the original structure lacked can add cost. Ordinance or law coverage pays for increased costs of code compliance up to its limit, but code requirements alone do not create coverage, so check your policy.</p>

<p>On the coast there is one more layer. Under Texas Insurance Code §2210.251, a structure that is built, altered, remodeled, enlarged, or repaired on or after January 1, 1988 needs a certificate of compliance to be eligible for TWIA coverage. If you rely on TWIA for wind and hail, structural fire repairs may need TDI windstorm inspection so you can keep that coverage. Those costs should be considered in the estimate. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has more detail.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Fire-Insurance-Adjuster-6-1024x683.webp" alt="Public adjuster reviewing an itemized fire claim estimate with a homeowner at a table inside a fire-damaged house" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Reviewing the estimate line by line with the owner helps confirm that smoke cleaning, contents, and code items are all accounted for.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Why fire claims are often underpaid</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Hidden damage.</strong> Smoke behind walls, heat-damaged wiring, and contaminated ductwork may not show up until demolition.</li>
<li><strong>Incomplete estimates.</strong> Missing cleaning steps, code items, permits, or contents categories.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation disputes.</strong> Disagreements over the age and condition of furniture, electronics, appliances, and building materials.</li>
<li><strong>Policy interpretation.</strong> Questions about additional living expenses, replacement cost, smoke remediation, and code upgrades.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you believe the insurer’s number is short, see our pages on <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>. Appraisal can resolve disputes over the amount of loss, not coverage.</p>

<h2>Commercial fire claims</h2>

<p>For businesses, the claim may include the building, tenant improvements, inventory, equipment, and business income. Restaurants, warehouses, and retail spaces each bring their own issues, from commercial kitchen equipment to smoke-damaged stock that cannot be sold. Business income claims depend heavily on financial records, so gather profit and loss statements, tax returns, and payroll records early. See our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help on a fire claim</h2>

<p>A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder. For a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, a fire claim that can mean coordinating the inspection, documenting structure and smoke damage, building the contents inventory, preparing an Xactimate estimate, reviewing the policy, and handling communication with the insurer, so you can focus on your family or your business. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. A public adjuster cannot give legal advice; for arson allegations, examinations under oath, or lawsuits, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

